Relational analysis

centers — triadic production [scope: altar] The Three Buddhas ensemble is the grammar's triadic production in its canonical form. Śākyamuni is the explicit hierarchical anchor — larger mandorla, central position, dominant scale — and Amitābha and Bhaiṣajyaguru are organized around him as equal lateral counterparts. The centers relation names what no binary pair of flanks or surmounts statements could fully capture: that the three figures constitute a single iconographic unit with a named centre, not a sequence of bilateral adjacencies.

mirrors — Amitābha and Bhaiṣajyaguru [scope: altar] The two flanking Buddhas are equal in scale, equivalent in compositional weight, and bilaterally symmetric across the central axis. Their mirroring encodes the cosmological completeness of the Three Buddhas programme: the three directions are balanced, the three temporal realms are equally represented, and the three soteriological domains are held in equipoise around the central figure of the historical Buddha.

mirrors — Ānanda and Mahākāśyapa [scope: altar] The attendant pair mirrors at a lower scale register, encoding the same bilateral symmetry at the level of human discipleship that the Three Buddhas encode at the level of cosmic Buddhahood. The nested mirroring — Buddhas mirroring Buddhas at one level, disciples mirroring disciples at another — creates a hierarchically layered bilateral programme that is among the most formally elaborate in this corpus.

faces — attendants toward Śākyamuni [scope: altar] The inward orientation of Ānanda and Mahākāśyapa is distinct from all other inward-facing figures analyzed in this corpus. The arhat groups in the Avalokiteśvara hall face inward as a collective assembly of liberated beings oriented toward a bodhisattva. The attendants here face inward as individual disciples in permanent attentiveness to their teacher. The relation encodes a specific pedagogical and relational bond: these figures are not merely spatially directed toward the centre but personally and historically bound to it through the transmission of the Dharma.

transmits — Śākyamuni to attendants The transmits relation here operates differently from the Avalokiteśvara–Maitreya transmits analyzed earlier, which encoded eschatological delegation across time. Here transmits encodes the historical transmission of the Dharma through personal discipleship: Śākyamuni transmitted his teaching to Ānanda through verbal recitation and to Mahākāśyapa through the famous wordless transmission of the flower sermon (niānhuā wēixiào 拈花微笑). Both forms of transmission are encoded in the same relation but distinguish themselves through the functional identity of each attendant — Ānanda the receiver of verbal teaching, Mahākāśyapa the receiver of direct mind-to-mind transmission.

opposes — Three Buddhas and Avalokiteśvara [scope: shrine] This is the most theologically consequential opposes relation in the corpus so far. At the Hall of Heavenly Kings, the opposes configuration between Maitreya and Skanda encoded a threshold-management programme: two figures covering the hall's two openings with complementary orientations. Here the opposes configuration encodes something different and more profound — a theological complementarity between awakening and compassion. The Three Buddhas face outward toward the arriving devotee, presenting the fullness of awakened reality as the goal and ground of the Buddhist path. Avalokiteśvara faces outward in the opposite direction, presenting the compassionate responsiveness that meets the devotee wherever they are in their journey, including at the monastery's rear threshold. Together they cover the hall's full axial extent — awakening at one end, compassion at the other — constituting the hall as a space in which the two inseparable aspects of the Buddhist path are simultaneously and permanently present.

intercedes — Avalokiteśvara toward Three Buddhas [scope: shrine] Avalokiteśvara's intercessory function within the hall operates shrine-internally as well as outward through the back threshold. She is positioned between the back entrance and the Three Buddhas — the devotee who approaches from the rear zone of the monastery encounters her intercessory presence before reaching the principal soteriological ensemble. Her intercedes relation directed toward the Three Buddhas encodes her mediating role: she receives the petitions of devotees approaching from the rear and channels them toward the Buddhas whose soteriological authority can address them.

History of religions analysis

The Three Buddhas as cosmogram The Three Buddhas ensemble is among the most explicit cosmograms in Chinese Buddhist iconography. Where the Thousand-Armed Avalokiteśvara in the shrine hall encodes a single deity's omnidirectional compassionate reach, the Three Buddhas encode the structure of the Buddhist cosmos itself: three temporal worlds (past, present, future in one reading; present world, western pure land, eastern lapis lazuli realm in another), three soteriological domains, three manifestations of Buddhahood held in perfect balance around a single centre. To stand before this ensemble is, in Eliade's terms, to stand at the imago mundi — the point where the structure of reality is made fully visible and spatially inhabitable.

The small Buddha figure embedded at the apex of Śākyamuni's mandorla deepens this reading. It encodes the principle of buddhakṣetra — the proliferation of Buddhas and Buddha-fields throughout infinite space — within the mandorla of the principal figure. The cosmos is not merely represented by the three figures; it is encoded within the central figure's own radiant field, the mandorla containing within itself a further instantiation of the Buddhahood it frames.

The opposes configuration as theological statement In van Gennep's analysis of liminal zones, the threshold figures on either side of a sacred boundary perform a rite of passage for whoever crosses between them. The opposes configuration in the Mahavira Hall extends this analysis: the devotee who enters from the front passes through the soteriological presence of the Three Buddhas; the devotee who enters from the rear passes through the intercessory compassion of Avalokiteśvara. The hall offers two modes of entry into its sacred programme, corresponding to two modes of approach to the Buddhist path — direct encounter with awakened reality (front entrance, Three Buddhas) and compassionate mediation for those not yet ready for direct encounter (rear entrance, Avalokiteśvara).

This is a theologically precise statement in Mahāyāna Buddhist terms. The prajñā (wisdom) of the Buddhas and the karuṇā (compassion) of the bodhisattva are the two inseparable wings of the Mahāyāna path. Their placement on opposite ends of the hall's central axis does not separate them — it encodes their complementary necessity. Neither alone is sufficient; both together constitute the full soteriological programme of the hall.

The attendants and the transmission of the Dharma Otto's phenomenology of the numinous addresses the experience of the sacred as mysterium tremendum et fascinans — overwhelming and compelling simultaneously. Ānanda and Mahākāśyapa perform a specific function within this phenomenology: they humanize it. Their white-robed, simplified forms standing at human scale before the towering golden Buddhas encode the possibility of human proximity to the overwhelming sacred presence. They have stood where the devotee now stands, received what the devotee now seeks, and transmitted what the devotee now approaches. Their inward-facing orientation invites the devotee to take their place in the lineage of transmission — to become, like them, a receiver and bearer of the teaching that radiates from the golden figure at the centre.
